NNAAMMEE
       pldd - display dynamic shared objects linked into a process

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
       pplldddd _p_i_d

       pplldddd _o_p_t_i_o_n

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       The  pplldddd  command displays a list of the dynamic shared objects (DSOs)
       that are linked into the process with the specified process  ID  (PID).
       The list includes the libraries that have been dynamically loaded using
       ddllooppeenn(3).

OOPPTTIIOONNSS
       ----hheellpp
       --??     Display a help message and exit.

       ----uussaaggee
              Display a short usage message and exit.

       ----vveerrssiioonn
       --VV     Display program version information and exit.

EEXXIITT SSTTAATTUUSS
       On  success,  pplldddd  exits  with the status 0.  If the specified process
       does not exist, the user does not have permission to access its dynamic
       shared object list, or no command‐line arguments are supplied, pplldddd ex‐
       ists with a status of 1.  If given an invalid option, it exits with the
       status 64.

VVEERRSSIIOONNSS
       Some other systems have a similar command.

SSTTAANNDDAARRDDSS
       None.

HHIISSTTOORRYY
       glibc 2.15.

NNOOTTEESS
       The command

           lsof -p PID

       also shows output that includes the dynamic  shared  objects  that  are
       linked into a process.

       The  ggddbb(1)  _i_n_f_o  _s_h_a_r_e_d command also shows the shared libraries being
       used by a process, so that one can obtain similar output to pplldddd  using
       a command such as the following (to monitor the process with the speci‐
       fied _p_i_d):

           $ ggddbb --eexx ""sseett ccoonnffiirrmm ooffff"" \\
                 --eexx ""sseett hheeiigghhtt 00"" \\
                 --eexx ""iinnffoo sshhaarreedd"" \\
                 --eexx ""qquuiitt"" \\
                 --pp $$ppiidd \\
           || ggrreepp ''^^00xx..**00xx'';

BBUUGGSS
       From  glibc 2.19 to glibc 2.29, pplldddd was broken: it just hung when exe‐
       cuted.  This problem was fixed in glibc 2.30,  and  the  fix  has  been
       backported to earlier glibc versions in some distributions.

